{"meta": {"limit": 500, "next": null, "offset": 0, "previous": null, "total_count": 65}, "objects": [{"abstract": "   The Routing Protocol for Low-Power and Lossy Networks (RPL) manages\n   the routing topology but lacks a mechanism to globally regulate how\n   many new nodes, known as Pledges, can join a node in a 6TiSCH network\n   at any given time.  Currently, Join Proxies (6LowPAN Routers) make\n   local decisions about whether to facilitate a Pledge's enrollment\n   based only on their immediate resources.\n\n   This document introduces RPL extensions to ensure that enrollment\n   remains orderly, prevents localized congestion at specific Join\n   Proxies, and allows the network to stay within its operational\n   capacity limits.\n", "ad": "/api/v1/person/person/122823/", "expires": "2027-01-22T14:34:47Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1730/", "id": 92886, "intended_std_level": "/api/v1/name/intendedstdlevelname/ps/", "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-ietf-roll-enrollment-priority", "note": "", "notify": "[email withheld by Lighthouse]", "pages": 13, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-ietf-roll-enrollment-priority/", "rev": "18", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": "/api/v1/person/email/[email withheld by Lighthouse]/", "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/112/", "/api/v1/doc/state/12/", "/api/v1/doc/state/44/"], "std_level": null, "stream": "/api/v1/name/streamname/ietf/", "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/106548/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/111066/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/111667/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/114602/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/117171/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/120862/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/124421/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/128324/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/133684/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/133736/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/138340/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/142676/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/150215/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/153313/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/157527/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/159048/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/165087/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/166060/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/166730/"], "tags": ["/api/v1/name/doctagname/need-rev/"], "time": "2026-08-20T14:15:36Z", "title": "Controlling Network Enrollment in RPL networks", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "This memo describes an update to the SMTP protocol, and to the format of an Internet mail message, to provide support for the character sets used in the world's many languages.  This memo documents existing usage as well as specifying some additional interoperability refinements.  It updates RFCs 821, 822, and 1090.                                                                                                                              The Internet is no longer a creature of the United States, much less of DARPA (the US Defence Advance Research Projects Agency). It is now an international network, and the ability to communicate in any of the world languages on an equal footing is an imperative.", "ad": null, "expires": "1992-04-20T07:00:01Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 16863, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-prime-ullmann-smtp", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 8, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-prime-ullmann-smtp/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": [], "tags": [], "time": "1991-10-18T07:00:00Z", "title": "International character support in SMTP", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "The Internet is no longer a creature of the United States, much less of DARPA (the US Defence Advance Research Projects Agency).  It is now an international network, and the ability to communicate in any of the world languages on an equal footing is an imperative.                            This draft attempts to track the development of ISO 10646, a moving target at this writing.  The reference citation below is to the 2nd 10646 draft, for which balloting has just concluded.  (June 1992).  It is therefore expected that this memo will potentially change until the publication of IS 10646.  Some of the following text refers to 10646 in the present tense, as if it is IS now; it should be understood in this context.", "ad": null, "expires": "1992-12-19T08:00:00Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 16973, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-ullmann-net-utf", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 10, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-ullmann-net-utf/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": [], "tags": [], "time": "1992-06-17T07:00:00Z", "title": "NET-UTF: International character set", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": 3123}, {"abstract": "This memo presents the specification for version 7 of the Internet Protocol, as well as version 7 of the TCP and the user datagram protocol.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.  It does not specify an Internet standard.", "ad": null, "expires": "2002-12-27T08:00:02Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1317/", "id": 17007, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-ullmann-ipv7", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 35, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-ullmann-ipv7/", "rev": "03", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/3/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": "/api/v1/name/stdlevelname/hist/", "stream": "/api/v1/name/streamname/ietf/", "submissions": [], "tags": [], "time": "2013-03-02T16:55:34Z", "title": "TP/IX: The Next Internet", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": 11401}, {"abstract": "This document describes NAP-based addressing, a scheme for abstracting and encoding hierarchical addresses in a name service in such a way as to allow the entities responsible for various levels in an address to change  their portions independently and scaleably.   As a byproduct, it also defines a particular addressing scheme which the author believes to be  appropriate for the existing model of Internet connectivity.", "ad": null, "expires": "1993-07-18T07:00:00Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 17091, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-wollman-nap-based", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 10, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-wollman-nap-based/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": [], "tags": [], "time": "1993-01-14T08:00:00Z", "title": "A Strategy for Encoding Hierarchical Addresses in Internet Name Services", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "This RFC describes an open distance vector routing protocol for use at all levels of the internet, from isolated LANs to the major routers of an international commercial network provider.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.  It does not specify an Internet standard.", "ad": null, "expires": "1993-09-25T07:00:01Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1317/", "id": 17111, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-ullmann-rap", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 20, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-ullmann-rap/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/3/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": "/api/v1/name/stdlevelname/exp/", "stream": "/api/v1/name/streamname/ietf/", "submissions": [], "tags": [], "time": "2013-03-02T16:55:34Z", "title": "RAP: Internet Route Access Protocol", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": 6759}, {"abstract": "This memo defines an additional MIME content-type defined in Message\r\nDisposition Notifications (MDN) that may be used by a mail user agent\r\n(UA) which is capable of Internet Fax Full Mode with Capabilities\r\nexchange and Confirmation of receipt. This content-type is intended to\r\nbe machine-processable. Additional message headers are also defined to\r\npermit that the sender (of the message) requires Message Disposition\r\nNotifications (MDNs). MDN is 'An Extensible Message Format for Message\r\nDisposition Notification' in RFC2298. Extension of MDN supports\r\nInternet fax feature tags defined in schema document[RFC-SCHEMA].\r\n", "ad": null, "expires": "1999-08-30T07:00:03Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1054/", "id": 20234, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-ietf-fax-mdn-fullmode", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 25, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-ietf-fax-mdn-fullmode/", "rev": "03", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/", "/api/v1/doc/state/38/"], "std_level": null, "stream": "/api/v1/name/streamname/ietf/", "submissions": [], "tags": [], "time": "1999-02-26T08:00:00Z", "title": "Extended MDN for Internet Fax Full Mode", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": 3837}, {"abstract": "This memo explains Internet Fax Full Mode facsimile applications\r\nwhich can be implemented using the method of capability exchange in\r\nInternet Fax Full Mode.  These applications are the same as\r\nimplemented in G3FAX using T.30 protocol. These applications will be\r\nimplemented under the Internet Draft 'Extended MDN for Internet Fax\r\nFull Mode' or other capability exchange mechanism.\r\n", "ad": null, "expires": "1999-01-29T08:00:00Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1054/", "id": 20390, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-ietf-fax-fullmode-application", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 12, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-ietf-fax-fullmode-application/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/", "/api/v1/doc/state/38/"], "std_level": null, "stream": "/api/v1/name/streamname/ietf/", "submissions": [], "tags": [], "time": "1998-07-28T07:00:00Z", "title": "Facsimile Applications for Internet Fax Full Mode", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": 2525}, {"abstract": "This memo defines a mechanism and formats for the Internet FAX Full\r\nMode using a MIME content-type and an extended Message Disposition\r\nNotifications (MDN).  Both of the MIME content-type and the extended\r\nMDN should be used by a mail user agent (UA) which is capable of\r\nInternet Fax Full Mode with capabilities exchange and confirmation of\r\nreceipt.\r\n \r\nThe MIME content-type that is used by Internet Fax Full Mode capable\r\nagent (UA) or electronic mail gateway to request, to response, and to\r\nreceive the capabilities of recipient.  This content-type is intended\r\nto be machine-processable.  Additional message headers are also defined\r\nto permit capabilities exchange in the Internet Fax Full Mode.\r\n \r\nThe extended Message Disposition Notifications (MDN) that is used by a\r\nmail user agent (UA) which is capable of Internet Fax Full Mode for\r\nprocessing confirmation. This content-type is intended to be\r\nmachine-processable.  Additional message headers are also defined to\r\npermit that the sender (of the message) requires Message Disposition\r\nNotifications (MDNs). MDN is 'An Extensible Message Format for Message\r\nDisposition Notification' in RFC2298.\r\n", "ad": null, "expires": "1999-04-23T07:00:01Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1054/", "id": 20584, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-ietf-fax-mime-fullmode", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 29, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-ietf-fax-mime-fullmode/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/", "/api/v1/doc/state/38/"], "std_level": null, "stream": "/api/v1/name/streamname/ietf/", "submissions": [], "tags": [], "time": "1998-10-20T07:00:00Z", "title": "MIME content-type for Internet Fax Full Mode", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": 4252}, {"abstract": "This document proposes two new TCP mechanisms that can be used to\r\nmore effectively recover lost segments when a connection's\r\ncongestion window is small, or when a large number of segments are\r\nlost in a single transmission window.  The first of these\r\nmechanisms, 'Limited Transmit', calls for sending a new data\r\nsegment in response to each of the first two duplicate\r\nacknowledgments that arrive at the sender.  The second mechanism is\r\nto reduce, in certain special circumstances, the number of duplicate\r\nacknowledgments required to trigger a fast retransmission.", "ad": null, "expires": "2000-12-07T08:00:00Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 22555, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-allman-tcp-lossrec", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 9, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-allman-tcp-lossrec/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": [], "tags": [], "time": "2000-06-05T07:00:00Z", "title": "Enhancing TCP's Loss Recovery Using Early Duplicate Acknowledgment Response\r\n", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": 3279}, {"abstract": "This document proposes a small modification to the way TCP increases its congestion window.  Rather than the traditional method of increasing the congestion window by a constant amount for each arriving acknowledgment, the document suggests basing the increase on the number of previously unacknowledged bytes each ACK covers.  This change improves the performance of TCP, as well as closes a security hole TCP receivers can use to induce the sender into increasing the sending rate too rapidly.  This memo defines an Experimental Protocol for the Internet community.", "ad": "/api/v1/person/person/2515/", "expires": "2003-05-08T07:00:04Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1324/", "id": 22711, "intended_std_level": "/api/v1/name/intendedstdlevelname/exp/", "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-allman-tcp-abc", "note": "Rev was made for tiny bug fix after good WGLC in TSVWG.  On 2002-Nov-27 IESG agenda.", "notify": "", "pages": 10, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-allman-tcp-abc/", "rev": "04", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/3/", "/api/v1/doc/state/7/"], "std_level": "/api/v1/name/stdlevelname/exp/", "stream": "/api/v1/name/streamname/ietf/", "submissions": [], "tags": ["/api/v1/name/doctagname/app-min/"], "time": "2015-10-14T20:50:03Z", "title": "TCP Congestion Control with Appropriate Byte Counting (ABC)", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": 3351}, {"abstract": "This document presents a conservative loss recovery algorithm for TCP that is based on the use of the selective acknowledgment (SACK) TCP option.  The algorithm presented in this document conforms to the spirit of the current congestion control specification (RFC 2581), but allows TCP senders to recover more effectively when multiple segments are lost from a single flight of data. [STANDARDS-TRACK]", "ad": "/api/v1/person/person/2324/", "expires": "2003-04-12T07:00:13Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1463/", "id": 23210, "intended_std_level": "/api/v1/name/intendedstdlevelname/ps/", "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-allman-tcp-sack", "note": "", "notify": "<[email withheld by Lighthouse]>, <[email withheld by Lighthouse]>", "pages": 13, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-allman-tcp-sack/", "rev": "13", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/3/", "/api/v1/doc/state/7/"], "std_level": "/api/v1/name/stdlevelname/ps/", "stream": "/api/v1/name/streamname/ietf/", "submissions": [], "tags": ["/api/v1/name/doctagname/app-min/"], "time": "2013-03-02T16:57:47Z", "title": "A Conservative Selective Acknowledgment (SACK)-based Loss Recovery Algorithm for TCP", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": 3652}, {"abstract": "This Internet draft is an attempt to describe security threats\r\nagainst the Rserpool protocol.  This draft presents requirements for\r\na security solution to thwart these threats in environments where it\r\nis likely to be deployed.  The threats and requirements identified\r\nherein and the document should be considered as work in progress.", "ad": null, "expires": "2002-09-02T07:00:02Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 24949, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-stillman-rserpool-threats", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 8, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-stillman-rserpool-threats/", "rev": "02", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": [], "tags": [], "time": "2002-03-01T08:00:00Z", "title": "Threats Introduced by Rserpool and Requirements for Security in response to Threats", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": 4395}, {"abstract": "This document standardises a new media type -- application/cellml+xml -- for use in exchanging mathematical models represented in a CellML Umbrella 1.0 compliant markup language.  This memo provides information for the Internet community.", "ad": "/api/v1/person/person/22971/", "expires": "2007-01-13T18:20:06Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1008/", "id": 32469, "intended_std_level": "/api/v1/name/intendedstdlevelname/inf/", "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-miller-media-type-cellml", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 7, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-miller-media-type-cellml/", "rev": "05", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/3/", "/api/v1/doc/state/7/"], "std_level": "/api/v1/name/stdlevelname/inf/", "stream": "/api/v1/name/streamname/ietf/", "submissions": [], "tags": ["/api/v1/name/doctagname/app-min/"], "time": "2015-10-14T20:51:29Z", "title": "CellML Media Type", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": 1631}, {"abstract": "This memo enumerates requirements to realize full-mesh multiparty \r\nconferencing using baseline Session Initiation Protocol. Primary \r\nrequirement is to advertise conference members’ addresses to new \r\nparticipants by concatenating multiple SDP session descriptions \r\ntogether. This particular requirement can solve several well-known \r\nproblems, including disjoint meshes and race conditions, that are \r\nlingering in full-mesh conferencing for a while now. ", "ad": null, "expires": "2003-11-30T08:00:00Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 27719, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-victor-fullmesh-reqts", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 7, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-victor-fullmesh-reqts/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": [], "tags": [], "time": "2003-05-29T07:00:00Z", "title": "Requirements for Full-mesh Conference Model using Baseline Session Initiation Protocol", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": 2375}, {"abstract": "This document sketches a potential quality control mechanism for the\r\nIETF in the form of working group review committees.  The idea is to\r\nform a small committee per working group that will provide document\r\nreview and advice throughout the working group's lifetime.  ", "ad": null, "expires": "2004-04-22T07:00:00Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 28377, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-allman-problem-wg-revcomm", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 4, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-allman-problem-wg-revcomm/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": [], "tags": [], "time": "2003-10-20T07:00:00Z", "title": "Using Working Group Review Committees", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": 1461}, {"abstract": "This document defines an experimental protocol called the Server Load \r\nBalancing Registration Protocol (SLBRP).  This protocol permits \r\nservers to register and coordinate services with a service load \r\nbalancing (SLB) application.  Coordination between real servers and \r\nthe SLB application will include service definition(s), Virtual IP \r\n(VIP) address support, server application health monitoring, as well \r\nas, optional support for automatic Domain Name System (DNS) and DNS-\r\nbased Global Server Load Balancer (GSLB) updates.  This document also \r\ndescribes Anycast Address Resolution Protocol (ARP) implementations \r\nthat can operate with an SLB and GSLB based enterprise \r\ninfrastructure. \r\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2004-06-10T07:00:00Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 28669, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-wollman-slbrp", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 33, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-wollman-slbrp/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": [], "tags": [], "time": "2003-12-08T08:00:00Z", "title": "Server Load Balancing Registration Protocol", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": 10826}, {"abstract": "This document sketches a potential quality control mechanism for the\r\n    IETF in the form of working group review committees.  The idea is to\r\n    form a small committee per working group that will provide document\r\n    review and advice throughout the working group's lifetime.", "ad": null, "expires": "2004-10-24T07:00:00Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 29220, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-allman-icar-wg-revcomm", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 5, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-allman-icar-wg-revcomm/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": [], "tags": [], "time": "2004-04-22T07:00:00Z", "title": "Using Working Group Review Committees", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": 1723}, {"abstract": "   When the 5321.MailFrom domain in an e-mail message has a DNS MX\n   Resource Record (RR), it is making an explicit statement that it is\n   willing to accept email.  However, when the domain has just a DNS A\n   or AAAA RR, there mail clients cannot easily tell whether the domain\n   accepts mail, as many hosts on the Internet advertise an A or AAAA RR\n   regardless of whether they want to accept email.\n\n   The NULL MX RR formalizes the existing mechanism by which a domain\n   announces that it accepts no mail.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2014-07-07T21:10:58Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1805/", "id": 30712, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-delany-nullmx", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 5, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-delany-nullmx/", "rev": "02", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": "/api/v1/person/email/[email withheld by Lighthouse]/", "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/4/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/", "/api/v1/doc/state/36/"], "std_level": null, "stream": "/api/v1/name/streamname/ietf/", "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/51723/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/56608/"], "tags": [], "time": "2014-01-30T23:55:48Z", "title": "A NULL MX Resource Record for Domains that Accept No Mail", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": 1642}, {"abstract": "DomainKeys Identified Mail (DKIM) defines a domain-level\r\n   authentication framework for email using public-key cryptography and\r\n   key server technology to permit verification of the source and\r\n   contents of messages by either Mail Transfer Agents (MTAs) or Mail\r\n   User Agents (MUAs).  The ultimate goal of this framework is to permit\r\n   a signing domain to assert responsibility for a message, thus proving\r\n   and protecting message sender identity and the integrity of the\r\n   messages they convey while retaining the functionality of Internet\r\n   email as it is known today.  Proof and protection of email identity,\r\n   including repudiation and non-repudiation, may assist in the global\r\n   control of \"spam\" and \"phishing\".", "ad": null, "expires": "2006-04-29T07:00:01Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 31095, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-allman-dkim-base", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 52, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-allman-dkim-base/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/4/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": [], "tags": [], "time": "2006-07-17T07:00:00Z", "title": "DomainKeys Identified Mail (DKIM)", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": 16102}, {"abstract": "DomainKeys Identified Mail (DKIM) defines a domain-level\r\nauthentication framework for email using public-key cryptography and\r\nkey server technology to permit verification of the source and\r\ncontents of messages by either Mail Transport Agents (MTAs) or Mail\r\nUser Agents (MUAs).  The primary DKIM protocol is described in\r\nThis document describes the records that senders may use to advertise\r\nhow they sign their outgoing mail, and how verifiers should access\r\nand interpret those results.", "ad": null, "expires": "2007-03-04T08:00:02Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 31098, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-allman-dkim-ssp", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 18, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-allman-dkim-ssp/", "rev": "02", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/4/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": [], "tags": [], "time": "2006-08-31T07:00:00Z", "title": "DKIM Sender Signing Practices", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": 5166}, {"abstract": "This document introduces a new EAP Method intended to automatically\r\nenroll clients with minimal user interaction in a wireless LAN\r\nenvironment.  The enrollment process involves the client providing\r\nweak possibly temporary credentials and the server providing stronger\r\npersistent credentials, both stages over a TLS-protected channel.\r\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2006-09-08T07:00:01Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 31217, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-mahy-eap-enrollment", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 21, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-mahy-eap-enrollment/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": [], "tags": [], "time": "2006-03-07T08:00:00Z", "title": "An Extensible Authentication Protocol (EAP) Enrollment Method", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": 6316}, {"abstract": "    This document describes a method for using spurious retransmission\r\n    timeouts as the trigger for slightly changing the way TCP's\r\n    retransmission timeout is computed in an effort to avoid subsequent\r\n    unnecessary retransmissions.\r\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2008-01-13T08:00:05Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 31345, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-allman-rto-backoff", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 8, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-allman-rto-backoff/", "rev": "05", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": [], "tags": [], "time": "2007-07-12T07:00:00Z", "title": "Using Spurious Retransmissions to Adapt the Retransmission Timeout", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": 2426}, {"abstract": "   This document describes how the IPv6 Neighbor Discovery messages with\r\n   link-local scope could be delivered with multicast CIDs in the 802.16\r\n   networks.", "ad": null, "expires": "2006-09-01T07:00:00Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 32278, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-jang-16ng-llm", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 12, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-jang-16ng-llm/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": [], "tags": [], "time": "2006-02-28T08:00:00Z", "title": "Link-local Multicast Packet Transmission in 802.16 Networks", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": 2626}, {"abstract": "   The memo has recommended to deploy Link-Local Multicast Name\n   Resolution (LLMNR) in PCP scenarioes.  Depending on that, it could\n   not only avoid adherence to DNS during PCP server name resolving, but\n   also company with PCP FQDN DHCP options extention to accomplish PCP\n   server discovery.  In order to fit LLMNR into PCP network, particular\n   LLMNR deployment guide and relevant configurations are considerated\n   along with PCP elements installation.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2012-05-03T07:00:01Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 39700, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-chen-pcp-discovery-llmnr", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 7, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-chen-pcp-discovery-llmnr/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/27367/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/36863/"], "tags": [], "time": "2012-05-03T07:21:39Z", "title": "Link-Local Multicast Name Resolution (LLMNR) Deployment Consideration for PCP Server Discovery", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": 1714}, {"abstract": "This document specifies the progression of initial TCP congestion\n window sizes over the next nine years.", "ad": null, "expires": "2011-05-19T07:00:00Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 39890, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-allman-tcpm-bump-initcwnd", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 4, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-allman-tcpm-bump-initcwnd/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/28399/"], "tags": [], "time": "2010-11-15T08:00:00Z", "title": "Initial Congestion Window Specification", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": 976}, {"abstract": "    Each implementation of a retransmission timeout mechanism must\n    balance correctness and timeliness and therefore no implementation\n    is suits all situations.  This document provides for high-level\n    guidance for retransmission timeout schemes appropriate for general\n    use in the Internet.  Within the guidelines, implementations have\n    latitude to define particulars that best address each situation.\n\nTerminology", "ad": null, "expires": "2016-05-27T03:01:24Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 40269, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-allman-tcpm-rto-consider", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 7, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-allman-tcpm-rto-consider/", "rev": "03", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/4/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/30544/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/41542/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/73865/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/74260/"], "tags": [], "time": "2016-05-26T07:07:37Z", "title": "Retransmission Timeout Considerations", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": 2197}, {"abstract": "   This document provides a sketch of a rendezvous protocol that allows\n   constrained internet devices such as sensors to securely connect into\n   a system that uses them.  The solution is based on the idea that each\n   device will be manufactured with a one time password that can be used\n   by the customer to tell the device which controller to enroll with,\n   and the device will be manufactured to contact a given Transfer\n   Server that is used to tell the device which system to connect to.\n   The administrator of the device will be able to get this one time\n   password from the device using a QR code, and then will be able to\n   use that one time password to inform a Transfer Server which system\n   the device should connect to.  The device will contact the Transfer\n   Agent, get this information, and then connect to the appropriate\n   system.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2013-04-16T14:40:15Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 48374, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-jennings-core-transitive-trust-enrollment", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 15, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-jennings-core-transitive-trust-enrollment/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/45150/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/45151/"], "tags": [], "time": "2013-04-16T07:24:32Z", "title": "Transitive Trust Enrollment for Constrained Devices", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": 4924}, {"abstract": "Internet mail determines the address of a receiving server through the DNS, first by looking for an MX record and then by looking for an A/AAAA record as a fallback.  Unfortunately, this means that the A/AAAA record is taken to be mail server address even when that address does not accept mail.  The No Service MX RR, informally called \"null MX\", formalizes the existing mechanism by which a domain announces that it accepts no mail, without having to provide a mail server; this permits significant operational efficiencies.", "ad": "/api/v1/person/person/21684/", "expires": "2015-03-17T19:01:45Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1805/", "id": 52245, "intended_std_level": "/api/v1/name/intendedstdlevelname/ps/", "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-ietf-appsawg-nullmx", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 6, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-ietf-appsawg-nullmx/", "rev": "10", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": "/api/v1/person/email/[email withheld by Lighthouse]/", "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/3/", "/api/v1/doc/state/108/", "/api/v1/doc/state/115/", "/api/v1/doc/state/7/", "/api/v1/doc/state/44/"], "std_level": "/api/v1/name/stdlevelname/ps/", "stream": "/api/v1/name/streamname/ietf/", "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/58205/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/59988/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/60049/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/60257/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/60558/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/60823/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/62002/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/62158/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/62229/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/62828/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/62829/"], "tags": [], "time": "2022-10-22T07:15:20Z", "title": "A \"Null MX\" No Service Resource Record for Domains That Accept No Mail", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": 1786}, {"abstract": "   Traditional discrete logarithm-based Diffie-Hellman (DH) key exchange\n   during the TLS handshake suffers from a number of security,\n   interoperability, and efficiency shortcomings.  These shortcomings\n   arise from lack of clarity about which DH group parameters TLS\n   servers should offer and clients should accept.  This document offers\n   a solution to these shortcomings for compatible peers by establishing\n   a registry of DH parameters with known structure and a mechanism for\n   peers to indicate support for these groups.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2014-10-30T22:55:34Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 55199, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-gillmor-tls-negotiated-dl-dhe", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 18, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-gillmor-tls-negotiated-dl-dhe/", "rev": "02", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/4/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/59025/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/59200/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/59623/"], "tags": [], "time": "2014-11-10T08:14:41Z", "title": "Negotiated Discrete Log Diffie-Hellman Ephemeral Parameters for TLS", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": 5453}, {"abstract": "    This specification removes the specification of TCP's initial\n    congestion window.\n\nTerminology", "ad": null, "expires": "2016-05-27T16:41:17Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 63986, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-allman-tcpm-no-initwin", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 3, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-allman-tcpm-no-initwin/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/74276/"], "tags": [], "time": "2016-05-27T07:07:44Z", "title": "Removing TCP's Initial Congestion Window", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": 795}, {"abstract": "   This document provides an overview of enrollment or imprinting\n   mechanisms in current IETF protocols.\n\n   This document is being worked on in the ANIMA-WG, and on github at:\n   https://github.com/anima-wg/enrollment-roadmap\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2021-04-11T02:46:06Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 77705, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-richardson-enrollment-roadmap", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 15, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-richardson-enrollment-roadmap/", "rev": "03", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/92719/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/92492/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/95421/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/114789/"], "tags": [], "time": "2021-04-10T07:26:12Z", "title": "Device Enrollment in IETF protocols -- A Roadmap", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   [I-D.ietf-6tisch-enrollment-enhanced-beacon] defines a method by\n   which a potential [I-D.ietf-6tisch-minimal-security] join proxy can\n   announce itself as a available for new Pledges to Join a network.\n   The announcement includes a priority for join.  This document\n   provides a mechanism by which a RPL DODAG root can disable join\n   announcements, or adjust the base priority for join operation.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2020-02-09T21:15:57Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1903/", "id": 82311, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-richardson-6tisch-roll-enrollment-priority", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 6, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-richardson-6tisch-roll-enrollment-priority/", "rev": "03", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/4/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/", "/api/v1/doc/state/134/"], "std_level": null, "stream": "/api/v1/name/streamname/ietf/", "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/92639/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/97397/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/101215/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/105929/"], "tags": [], "time": "2019-08-08T20:15:57Z", "title": "Enabling secure network enrollment in RPL networks", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   In TSCH mode of IEEE802.15.4, as described by [RFC8180],\n   opportunities for broadcasts are limited to specific times and\n   specific channels.  Nodes in a TSCH network typically frequently send\n   Enhanced Beacon (EB) frames to announce the presence of the network.\n   This document provides a mechanism by which small details critical\n   for new nodes (pledges) and long sleeping nodes may be carried within\n   the Enhanced Beacon.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2018-10-26T19:42:31Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1903/", "id": 82357, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-richardson-6tisch-enrollment-enhanced-beacon", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 8, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-richardson-6tisch-enrollment-enhanced-beacon/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/4/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/", "/api/v1/doc/state/36/"], "std_level": null, "stream": "/api/v1/name/streamname/ietf/", "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/92754/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/94974/"], "tags": [], "time": "2018-07-20T20:51:22Z", "title": "IEEE802.15.4 Informational Element encapsulation of 6tisch Join and Enrollment Information", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   In TSCH mode of IEEE802.15.4, as described by [RFC8180],\n   opportunities for broadcasts are limited to specific times and\n   specific channels.  Nodes in a TSCH network typically frequently send\n   Enhanced Beacon (EB) frames to announce the presence of the network.\n   This document provides a mechanism by which small details critical\n   for new nodes (pledges) and long sleeping nodes may be carried within\n   the Enhanced Beacon.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2019-01-18T06:03:37Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 85558, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-6tisch-enrollment-enhanced-beacon", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 8, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-6tisch-enrollment-enhanced-beacon/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/97060/"], "tags": [], "time": "2019-01-17T08:17:35Z", "title": "IEEE802.15.4 Informational Element encapsulation of 6tisch Join and Enrollment Information", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "In the Time-Slotted Channel Hopping (TSCH) mode of IEEE Std 802.15.4, opportunities for broadcasts are limited to specific times and specific channels.  Routers in a TSCH network transmit Enhanced Beacon (EB) frames to announce the presence of the network.  This document provides a mechanism by which additional information critical for new nodes (pledges) and long-sleeping nodes may be carried within the EB in order to conserve use of broadcast opportunities.", "ad": "/api/v1/person/person/106412/", "expires": "2020-08-24T19:28:06Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1903/", "id": 86287, "intended_std_level": "/api/v1/name/intendedstdlevelname/ps/", "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-ietf-6tisch-enrollment-enhanced-beacon", "note": "", "notify": "Pascal Thubert <[email withheld by Lighthouse]>", "pages": 10, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-ietf-6tisch-enrollment-enhanced-beacon/", "rev": "14", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": "/api/v1/person/email/[email withheld by Lighthouse]/", "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/3/", "/api/v1/doc/state/108/", "/api/v1/doc/state/154/", "/api/v1/doc/state/115/", "/api/v1/doc/state/7/", "/api/v1/doc/state/44/"], "std_level": "/api/v1/name/stdlevelname/ps/", "stream": "/api/v1/name/streamname/ietf/", "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/97065/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/100824/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/102742/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/106387/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/106408/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/106547/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/108081/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/109250/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/109284/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/109356/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/109737/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/109763/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/109778/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/109791/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/109904/"], "tags": [], "time": "2021-05-30T04:42:22Z", "title": "Encapsulation of 6TiSCH Join and Enrollment Information Elements", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document describes a secure key exchange scheme that extends the\n   Time-Based One-Time Password (TOTP) [RFC6238] de facto enrollment\n   method to prevent compromise of the non-expiring TOTP key by\n   photographic capture of the QR code or by intentional or\n   unintentional persistence of the key string in email, SMS, or other\n   systems outside of the TOTP authenticator system itself.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-03-31T00:30:24Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 139411, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-contario-totp-secure-enrollment", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 21, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-contario-totp-secure-enrollment/", "rev": "02", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/150811/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/145808/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/155541/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-03-30T07:09:00Z", "title": "TOTP Secure Enrollment", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "This document proposes a new mechanism for TCP and SCTP that can be\r\n    used to recover lost segments when a connection's congestion window\r\n    is small.  The \"Early Retransmit\" mechanism allows the transport to\r\n    reduce, in certain special circumstances, the number of duplicate\r\n    acknowledgments required to trigger a fast retransmission.  This\r\n    allows the transport to use fast retransmit to recover packet losses\r\n    that would otherwise require a lengthy retransmission timeout.", "ad": "/api/v1/person/person/112773/", "expires": "2008-12-28T01:45:36Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1324/", "id": 27376, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-allman-tcp-early-rexmt", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 12, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-allman-tcp-early-rexmt/", "rev": "07", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": "/api/v1/name/streamname/ietf/", "submissions": [], "tags": ["/api/v1/name/doctagname/app-min/"], "time": "2024-12-18T18:43:40Z", "title": "Early Retransmit for TCP and SCTP", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": 4068}, {"abstract": "    In this document we aim to solve several problems caused by TCP's\r\n    lack of header space for certain values by increasing the size of\r\n    header without changing the semantics of the protocol.\r\n", "ad": "/api/v1/person/person/112773/", "expires": "2006-11-09T08:00:00Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1324/", "id": 32594, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-allman-tcpx2-hack", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 9, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-allman-tcpx2-hack/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": "/api/v1/name/streamname/ietf/", "submissions": [], "tags": ["/api/v1/name/doctagname/app-min/"], "time": "2024-12-18T18:43:49Z", "title": "TCPx2: Don't Fence Me In", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": 2912}, {"abstract": "   Large Language Models (LLMs) deployments are becoming increasingly\n   widespread, with inference services being the most common\n   application.  This draft will discuss multicast use cases for\n   inference cloud services.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-02-12T09:44:05Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 153637, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-liu-rtgwg-llmsync-multicast", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 6, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-liu-rtgwg-llmsync-multicast/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/159618/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/167570/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-08-11T09:44:05Z", "title": "Multicast Use Cases for Large Language Model Synchronization", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Agentic AI systems require robust, verifiable identities to operate\n   securely.  While traditional certificate enrollment protocols like\n   SCEP and EST can be extended to include remote attestation evidence,\n   performing a full validation for every agent's certificate request\n   presents significant scalability and privacy challenges.  This\n   document proposes two distinct, scalable models for integrating\n   attestation into the enrollment lifecycle.  The first model leverages\n   Zero-Knowledge Proofs (ZKP) to provide private, efficient, and\n   continuous attestation.  The second model uses a one-time, high-\n   assurance bootstrapping process to establish a trusted host\n   environment, which is then authorized to endorse certificate requests\n   for the agents it runs.  Both models enable high-assurance identity\n   for AI agents while addressing the performance bottlenecks of naive\n   per-enrollment verification.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2025-12-16T06:24:58Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 146471, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-huang-acme-scalable-agent-enrollment", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 9, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-huang-acme-scalable-agent-enrollment/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/152750/"], "tags": [], "time": "2025-12-16T07:09:03Z", "title": "Extending Certificate Enrollment Protocols for Scalable Agentic AI Identity", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document specifies an evaluation framework and related\n   definitions for intent-driven network configuration using Large\n   Language Model(LLM)-based agents.  The framework combines an\n   emulator-based interactive environment, a suite of representative\n   tasks, and multi-dimensional metrics organized in two layers: outcome\n   metrics that verify functional correctness in a method-agnostic way,\n   and agentic process metrics that assess reasoning quality and\n   interactive command generation.  Functional testcase results serve as\n   the primary metric; command and reasoning scores act as diagnostic\n   estimates against the task's ground truth configurations (which may\n   include more than one validated solution), and optional efficiency\n   metrics capture time and token cost.  The framework aims to enable\n   reproducible, comprehensive, and fair comparisons among network\n   configuration approaches, covering both agentic and non-agentic\n   solutions while highlighting capabilities specific to autonomous\n   agents.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-01-07T08:34:48Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 146700, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-cui-nmrg-llm-benchmark", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 29, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-cui-nmrg-llm-benchmark/", "rev": "02", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/153121/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/158375/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/165818/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-07-06T08:34:48Z", "title": "A Framework to Evaluate LLM Agents for Network Configuration", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Large Language Models (LLMs) have been widely used in recent years.\n   The Mixture of Experts (MoE) architecture is one of the features of\n   LLMs that enables efficient inference and cost-effective training.\n   With the MoE architecture, there are potential multicast use cases\n   such as tokens dispatching.  This draft attempts to analyze these use\n   cases.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-02-11T01:43:53Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 146851, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-zhang-rtgwg-llmmoe-multicast", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 7, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-zhang-rtgwg-llmmoe-multicast/", "rev": "03", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/153512/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/156507/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/167523/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/162147/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-08-10T01:43:53Z", "title": "Multicast use case in LLM MoE", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document defines an interoperable framework that facilitates\n   collaborative network management between Large Language Models (LLMs)\n   and human operators.  The proposed framework introduces enhanced\n   telemetry module, LLM decision module and standardized interaction\n   data models between human operators and LLM-driven systems, and\n   workflows to enforce human oversight.  The approach ensures\n   compatibility with existing network management systems and protocols\n   while improving automation and decision-making capabilities in\n   network operations.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2025-09-14T15:44:40Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/38/", "id": 143789, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-irtf-nmrg-llm-nm", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 12, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-irtf-nmrg-llm-nm/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/", "/api/v1/doc/state/57/"], "std_level": null, "stream": "/api/v1/name/streamname/irtf/", "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/149965/"], "tags": [], "time": "2025-09-14T07:09:00Z", "title": "A Framework for LLM-Assisted Network Management with Human-in-the-Loop", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Large Language Models (LLMs) are entering network operations through\n   natural-language intent interfaces.  Existing south-bound protocols\n   (NETCONF, RESTCONF, gNMI, MCP, A2A) were not designed for\n   conversational, semantically-rich, multi-agent orchestration.  This\n   document provides a systematic gap analysis and identifies extension\n   points for each protocol to meet intent-based networking\n   requirements.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-04-17T12:24:31Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 150917, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-zeng-opsawg-llm-netconf-gap", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 9, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-zeng-opsawg-llm-netconf-gap/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/157086/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/170347/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-14T12:24:31Z", "title": "Gap Analysis of Network Configuration Protocols in LLM-Driven Intent-Based Networking", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   With the rise of ChatGPT, DeepSeek, and other Large Language Models,\n   which is short for LLMs in the remaining part, as well as the\n   proliferation of inference applications, inference serving oriented\n   to large-scale users has become increasingly critical.  However, due\n   to the extreme demands on computing power and communication during\n   inference, the large-scale service deployment of LLMs poses\n   significant challenges.  To address these challenges, different\n   vendors have adopted diverse inference service architectures, such as\n   vLLM, SGLang, Mooncake, etc.  This paper investigates mainstream\n   inference frameworks, summarizes their core design principle and\n   research question, and analyzes the challenges and requirements they\n   impose on network management.  The goal is to lay a foundation for\n   defining a unified LLM inference architecture in the future.\n\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-05-08T02:55:18Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 143440, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-liu-nmrg-ai-llm-inference-requirements", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 10, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-liu-nmrg-ai-llm-inference-requirements/", "rev": "02", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/149989/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/153814/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/157258/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-05-07T07:09:07Z", "title": "Requirements Analysis of System and Network for Large Language Model Inference Service", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document defines terminology for benchmarking the performance of\n   Large Language Model (LLM) inference serving systems.  It establishes\n   a shared vocabulary for latency, throughput, resource utilization,\n   and quality metrics applicable to inference engines, application\n   gateways, and compound agentic systems.  This document defines\n   terminology only and does not prescribe benchmark methodologies or\n   acceptance thresholds.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-02-10T16:10:24Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 153276, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-gaikwad-llm-benchmarking-terminology", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 54, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-gaikwad-llm-benchmarking-terminology/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/158893/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/167505/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-08-09T16:10:24Z", "title": "Benchmarking Terminology for Large Language Model Serving", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document defines benchmarking methodologies for Large Language\n   Model (LLM) inference serving systems.  It provides test procedures,\n   setup parameters, measurement specifications, and reporting formats\n   for evaluating latency, throughput, scheduling, and resource\n   management characteristics.  This document is a companion to\n   \"Benchmarking Terminology for Large Language Model Serving\" and\n   SHOULD be consulted alongside that terminology document.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-02-10T16:11:09Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 153278, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-gaikwad-llm-benchmarking-methodology", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 48, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-gaikwad-llm-benchmarking-methodology/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/158900/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/167507/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-08-09T16:11:09Z", "title": "Benchmarking Methodology for Large Language Model Serving", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document defines performance benchmarking profiles for Large\n   Language Model (LLM) serving systems.  Profiles bind the terminology\n   defined in draft-gaikwad-llm-benchmarking-terminology and the\n   procedures described in draft-gaikwad-llm-benchmarking-methodology to\n   concrete architectural roles and workload patterns.  Each profile\n   clarifies the System Under Test (SUT) boundary, measurement points,\n   and interpretation constraints required for reproducible and\n   comparable benchmarking.\n\n   This document specifies profiles only.  It does not define new\n   metrics, benchmark workloads, or acceptance thresholds.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-02-10T16:10:56Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 153279, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-gaikwad-llm-benchmarking-profiles", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 53, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-gaikwad-llm-benchmarking-profiles/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/158904/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/167506/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-08-09T16:10:56Z", "title": "Performance Benchmarking Profiles for Large Language Model Serving Systems", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document defines the \"llm\" Uniform Resource Identifier (URI)\n   scheme for identifying Large Language Model (LLM) endpoints and their\n   configuration.  The scheme encodes provider host, model identifier,\n   authentication credentials, and inference parameters into a single,\n   portable connection string, analogous to database connection URIs\n   such as those used by PostgreSQL and MongoDB.\n\n   This document registers the scheme name in the IANA \"Uniform Resource\n   Identifier (URI) Schemes\" registry as a provisional registration per\n   RFC 7595.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-08-10T17:55:32Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 153531, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-levy-llm-uri-scheme", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 17, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-levy-llm-uri-scheme/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/159407/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-08-10T07:09:04Z", "title": "The \"llm\" URI Scheme", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document specifies a trust anchor bootstrap protocol for the\n   witnessd Proof of Process framework.  The protocol defines how new\n   devices enter the witnessd ecosystem, how device keys are provisioned\n   and verified, and how Verifiers discover and validate device trust\n   anchors.\n\n   Three enrollment modes are defined: self-sovereign mode for\n   individual users, organizational mode for enterprise deployments, and\n   public registry mode for federated trust ecosystems.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-08-11T10:31:34Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 153541, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-condrey-rats-witnessd-enrollment", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 13, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-condrey-rats-witnessd-enrollment/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/159435/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-08-11T07:09:05Z", "title": "Trust Anchor Bootstrap Protocol for Proof of Process", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document specifies CONDUIT, a tunnel fabric management protocol\n   for tactical and enterprise networks operating over heterogeneous\n   Wide Area Network (WAN) links.  CONDUIT automates the lifecycle\n   management of IPsec tunnels, monitors tunnel health through active\n   probing, and publishes quality metrics to enable Segment Routing over\n   IPv6 (SRv6) based traffic engineering.\n\n   CONDUIT follows a strict separation of concerns: it manages the\n   underlay tunnel fabric while delegating all traffic engineering\n   decisions to SRv6.  This architecture simplifies network operations,\n   enables rapid adaptation to changing WAN conditions, and leverages\n   standard SRv6 capabilities including Flexible Algorithm (Flex-Algo)\n   and Topology-Independent Loop-Free Alternate (TI-LFA).\n\n   The protocol is fully programmable via gRPC and mandates compliance\n   with Commercial National Security Algorithm Suite 2.0 (CNSA 2.0)\n   cryptographic requirements.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-08-24T04:16:31Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 153726, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-willman-rtgwg-conduit-tunnels", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 38, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-willman-rtgwg-conduit-tunnels/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/159871/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/159870/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-08-23T07:09:05Z", "title": "Underlay for IPsec Transport", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document specifies PRISM, an application-aware traffic steering\n   protocol for Software-Defined Wide Area Networks (SD-WAN).  PRISM\n   provides deep application identification, per-flow tracking, Service\n   Level Agreement (SLA) enforcement, and policy-based path selection\n   integration with Segment Routing over IPv6 (SRv6).\n\n   PRISM is designed as a companion protocol to CONDUIT (Cryptographic\n   Orchestration of Network Distributed Underlay for IPsec Transport),\n   together providing a complete open-standard SD-WAN solution.  CONDUIT\n   manages the encrypted tunnel fabric while PRISM provides the\n   application intelligence and policy enforcement.\n\n   The protocol is fully programmable via gRPC, supports distributed and\n   centralized deployment models, and mandates a phased transition to\n   Commercial National Security Algorithm Suite 2.0 (CNSA 2.0)\n   cryptographic requirements.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-09-17T20:27:37Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 154949, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-willman-rtgwg-prism-sdwan", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 22, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-willman-rtgwg-prism-sdwan/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/161178/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-09-17T07:09:15Z", "title": "PRISM: Protocol for Routing Intelligent Service Mapping", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document defines standard workload profiles for benchmarking\n   Large Language Model (LLM) inference serving systems.  Each profile\n   represents one class of production workload.  A profile is defined by\n   its input and output token distribution, output structure, latency\n   sensitivity, concurrency pattern, and caching behavior.  Profiles are\n   organized into six groups: Non-Generative, Minimal Output,\n   Interactive Streaming, Prefill-Heavy Generative, Decode-Heavy\n   Generative, and Multi-Step Chained.  This document works with\n   \"Benchmarking Methodology for Large Language Model Serving\"\n   [LLM-METHOD].  It specifies the workloads that the methodology's\n   tests SHOULD be run against.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-10-11T16:37:35Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 155964, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-mondal-llm-serving-workload-profiles", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 56, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-mondal-llm-serving-workload-profiles/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/162031/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-11T07:09:04Z", "title": "Benchmarking Workload Profiles for Large Language Model Serving", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Large Language Models (LLMs) are increasingly used to assist network\n   management tasks such as troubleshooting, intent translation, and\n   automation.  Network operations, however, rely on data from many\n   vendors and sources: CLI output, configuration snippets, telemetry,\n   alarms, and vendor-specific APIs.  These inputs differ in format,\n   structure, and semantics, which makes it difficult to present a\n   consistent interface to an LLM.  This document describes a framework\n   for standardizing such multi-vendor inputs for LLM-assisted network\n   management.  Incoming messages from multi-vendor network elements are\n   first handled by an Input Classifier, which determines the nature of\n   each input and assigns it to one of three categories: performance,\n   configuration, or response, using a hybrid approach (rule-based\n   classification first, with escalation to a Small Language Model (SLM)\n   when rules are insufficient).  The classified input is then passed to\n   the corresponding Structurer among the Performance Structurer,\n   Configuration Structurer, and Response Structurer, each of which\n   produces a normalized, structured representation (typically with SLM\n   assistance and optional confidence scoring).  That structured output\n   is fed to a Prompt Schema Generator, which creates a structured,\n   vendor-agnostic schema and supplies schema-aligned prompts to the\n   central LLM.  This document specifies the architecture and component\n   roles for use in design and implementation.  It does not define a\n   wire protocol; it is published for informational purposes.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-04-08T10:08:25Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 156538, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-prabhu-nmrg-prompt-schema-llm", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 15, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-prabhu-nmrg-prompt-schema-llm/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/162949/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/169854/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-05T10:08:25Z", "title": "Framework for Normalizing Multi-Vendor Network Inputs for LLM-Assisted Network Management", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document describes a reference framework for collaborative\n   network management between Large Language Model (LLM)-assisted agents\n   and human operators.  Because network management actions can affect\n   service availability, security posture, customer traffic, and\n   compliance obligations, LLM-generated recommendations need to be\n   validated, reviewed, and audited before they are applied to\n   operational networks.  The framework therefore focuses on human-in-\n   the-loop control for safe, auditable, and operator-supervised use of\n   LLM-assisted decision support in network operations.  The document is\n   intended to be compatible with existing network management systems\n   and protocols while identifying research issues, rather than\n   specifying a complete implementation of all LLM agent mechanisms.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-01-02T13:47:16Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 143444, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-cui-nmrg-llm-nm", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 18, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-cui-nmrg-llm-nm/", "rev": "02", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/150005/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/156389/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/165297/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-07-01T13:47:16Z", "title": "A Framework for LLM Agent-Assisted Network Management with Human-in-the-Loop", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   The Agent Enrollment Protocol (AEP) defines an HTTP-based mechanism\n   for autonomous agents to discover service enrollment requirements,\n   enroll an agent identity, obtain optional session credentials, revoke\n   those credentials, and query enrollment status.  AEP uses\n   Decentralized Identifiers, client assertion JWTs, and HTTP Problem\n   Details to provide a narrow machine-first enrollment and\n   authentication substrate for agent-to-service interactions.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-04T23:04:21Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 157522, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-kavian-agent-enrollment-protocol", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 44, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-kavian-agent-enrollment-protocol/", "rev": "05", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/171562/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/166848/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/168604/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/168010/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/165096/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/165056/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-31T23:04:21Z", "title": "The Agent Enrollment Protocol", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Large Language Model (LLM) inference endpoints stream response tokens\n   to clients using a fragmented set of vendor-specific application-\n   layer protocols layered on top of standardized transports (HTTP/2,\n   HTTP/1.1, WebSocket, Server-Sent Events).  While these transports\n   carry IETF or W3C standardization, the JSON payload schemas, event\n   taxonomies, and framing conventions used within them are entirely\n   vendor-defined, with no RFCs or common specifications governing them.\n\n   This fragmentation imposes costs across the AI ecosystem.  Middleware\n   frameworks and orchestration platforms (LangChain, LiteLLM, Vercel AI\n   SDK, Portkey, Cloudflare AI Gateway) must maintain vendor-specific\n   streaming parsers for every supported provider.  Cloud hosting\n   platforms (AWS Bedrock, Azure AI Studio, Google Vertex AI) have each\n   introduced additional proprietary streaming formats.  Compliance and\n   observability tooling must be rebuilt per provider.  And new\n   inference providers cannot reach framework-dependent developers\n   without custom integration work.\n\n   This document defines a standard wire format for LLM inference\n   streaming over Server-Sent Events (SSE) on HTTP.  It specifies a\n   request envelope media type (application/llm-request+json), a\n   response event taxonomy, and a JSON event envelope schema that enable\n   middleware, orchestration platforms, compliance tooling, and HTTP\n   intermediaries to handle AI inference traffic from any conforming\n   provider using a single protocol contract.  SSE transport remains\n   unchanged; only the JSON payload inside it is standardized.\n\n   The scope of this document is strictly Client/Application to LLM\n   inference endpoint streaming.  Agent-to-tool interaction (e.g., MCP)\n   and agent-to-agent communication are out of scope.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-01-20T08:11:42Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 158511, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-spk-agentproto-llm-stream", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 31, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-spk-agentproto-llm-stream/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/166417/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-07-19T08:11:42Z", "title": "A Standard Wire Format for Large Language Model Inference Streaming", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Any scheme that produces cryptographic evidence of execution\n   authority roots its entire trust chain in an enrollment: the moment\n   at which a key becomes bound to a subject and a device, and at which\n   a verifier begins to treat signatures under that key as meaningful.\n   Every downstream proof is only as trustworthy as that binding.  If an\n   attacker substitutes a key of their choosing at enrollment, every\n   subsequent proof verifies correctly and yet attests to the wrong\n   party.  This document is a problem statement.  It describes the\n   foundational key-substitution threat, records the relevant public\n   facts about consumer-platform biometric and key APIs that bound what\n   an enrollment can establish, and states the assumptions a verifier of\n   execution authority evidence must be able to make about the\n   enrollment its proofs depend on.  Specific enrollment ceremonies are\n   out of scope.  This document defines no protocol or mechanism.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-01-23T12:14:30Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 159484, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-yossif-enrollment-problem", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 9, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-yossif-enrollment-problem/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/166794/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-07-22T12:14:30Z", "title": "Problem Statement: Enrollment and Key-Binding Assumptions in Execution Authority Evidence", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Large Language Models (LLMs) are now accessed as network services by\r\n   a large and growing number of applications, yet the way they are\r\n   invoked, secured, described, and attributed has coalesced around\r\n   vendor-specific and de facto interfaces rather than open, neutral\r\n   standards.  The most consequential gap is in security: there is no\r\n   common, analyzable model for authenticating to an LLM service,\r\n   authorizing tool invocation, protecting and attributing output, or\r\n   reasoning about the trust boundary an LLM sits on.  These models also\r\n   sit directly on top of network storage: LLM training data, model\r\n   checkpoints, and retrieval corpora are routinely served from NFSv4\r\n   and pNFS, so the security, provenance, and access-control questions\r\n   raised here have concrete NFSv4-facing consequences.  This document\r\n   argues that uniting the multitude of competing LLMs behind a common,\r\n   secure, interoperable interface is fundamentally the kind of problem\r\n   the IETF exists to solve, and it makes the case for chartering a new\r\n   IETF Working Group.  It sets out the problem with security as the\r\n   central motivation, describes the interaction with NFSv4 and network\r\n   storage, records the initial expressions of interest that any such\r\n   effort must be able to show, proposes a candidate scope with concrete\r\n   deliverables and explicit non-goals, addresses the principal\r\n   objections, and describes the intended path to chartering through a\r\n   Dispatch presentation or a Birds-of-a-Feather (BOF) session.  This\r\n   document is Informational and defines no protocol.\r\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-01-28T16:51:45Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 160010, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-faibish-llm-security-interop", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 11, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-faibish-llm-security-interop/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/166934/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-07-27T16:51:45Z", "title": "The Case for an IETF Working Group on Large Language Model Security and Interoperability", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document defines test procedures for characterizing the fault\n   detection capability of observability systems that monitor Large\n   Language Model (LLM) serving deployments.  Procedures are given for\n   Detection Latency, Detection Coverage, Detection Threshold Magnitude,\n   False Detection Rate, and Boundary Masking.\n\n   The Detector Under Test is the observability system.  Output Behavior\n   Faults are injected at a known time under controlled conditions,\n   which makes detection latency directly measurable.\n\n   This document is a companion to \"Benchmarking Terminology for Output\n   Behavior Fault Detection in Large Language Model Serving Systems\" and\n   is to be read alongside it.  This document specifies no acceptance\n   thresholds.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-02-12T08:54:43Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 160324, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-gaikwad-llm-fault-detection-methodology", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 13, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-gaikwad-llm-fault-detection-methodology/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/167568/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-08-11T08:54:43Z", "title": "Benchmarking Methodology for Output Behavior Fault Detection in Large Language Model Serving Systems", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document defines terminology for reporting incidents in Large\n   Language Model (LLM) services in which output behavior departs from\n   intended behavior without producing an error response.  It defines\n   the lifecycle events of such an incident, the intervals between those\n   events, and the qualifiers a reported interval carries.\n\n   The intervals defined here are anchored at the point information\n   about an occurrence reaches a responsible party.  The interval\n   preceding the first monitoring signal is named and is not defined as\n   measurable.\n\n   This document defines terminology only.  It specifies no data model,\n   no reporting obligations, and no target values.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-02-12T09:36:58Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 160325, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-gaikwad-llm-incident-metrics-terminology", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 25, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-gaikwad-llm-incident-metrics-terminology/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/167569/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-08-11T09:36:58Z", "title": "Terminology for Reporting Output Behavior Incidents in Large Language Model Services", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document defines terminology for benchmarking the fault\n   detection capability of observability systems that monitor Large\n   Language Model (LLM) serving deployments.  It defines terms for\n   injectable output behavior fault classes, for the detection events\n   and latency intervals those faults produce, and for the coverage and\n   accuracy characteristics of the detector under test.\n\n   The System Under Test is the observability system, and not the\n   language model.  Faults are injected at a known time under controlled\n   conditions, which makes detection latency measurable in a way it is\n   not in production.\n\n   This document defines terminology only.  A companion document defines\n   the corresponding methodology.  This document specifies no test\n   procedures, no acceptance thresholds, and no service-level\n   objectives.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-02-12T07:37:31Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 160322, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-gaikwad-llm-fault-detection-terminology", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 14, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-gaikwad-llm-fault-detection-terminology/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/167562/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-08-11T07:37:31Z", "title": "Benchmarking Terminology for Output Behavior Fault Detection in Large Language Model Serving Systems", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Publishers have begun to serve a curated, plain-text summary of a web\n   origin intended for consumption by large language models and by the\n   crawlers that feed them, most visibly under the de facto file name\n   \"llms.txt\".  The practice is described by an informal proposal which,\n   in its current version, recommends existing link relations from\n   individual pages to the file.  It has no media type, no rules that\n   bound the cost of retrieval, and no way for a consumer that starts\n   from an origin's robots.txt or from a fixed well-known location to\n   learn that such a file exists.\n\n   This document specifies discovery and retrieval for publisher-curated\n   context files.  It defines the well-known URI \"llm-context\", the link\n   relation type \"llm-context\", and an extension record for the robots\n   exclusion protocol, so that a publisher may advertise a context file\n   by three independent paths and a consumer may find it without\n   guessing.  It specifies a two-tier arrangement of an index resource\n   and optional detail resources, states conditional-request and size\n   requirements that keep retrieval affordable for both parties, and\n   describes the relationship of this mechanism to the robots exclusion\n   protocol, to sitemaps, to the llms.txt proposal itself, and to work\n   in the IETF on AI usage preferences, agent communication and the\n   discovery of AI agents.\n\n   This document also reports measurements from an operational\n   deployment in which requests presenting twenty crawler tokens of\n   search and language-model providers numbered 46,155 over fifteen days\n   at one origin server, without once retrieving the context file the\n   origin was serving, while the same crawler tokens retrieved\n   robots.txt 698 times in the three days after the context file was\n   deployed.  The measurement figures in revision -00 were computed on\n   an incomplete log and are corrected here.  The absence of a discovery\n   mechanism, rather than the absence of interest, is the hypothesis\n   this document acts upon.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-04-08T13:41:56Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 160984, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-arsentev-llm-context-discovery", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 22, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-arsentev-llm-context-discovery/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/168908/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/169861/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-05T13:41:56Z", "title": "Discovery and Retrieval of Publisher-Curated Context Files for Large Language Model Consumers", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Large Language Model (LLM) API services can be hosted on\n   workstations, local servers, and other devices in a local network.\n   AI applications commonly access these services through manually\n   configured API base URLs and interface settings.  When an application\n   joins a different network or a service address changes, these\n   settings may need to be updated even when a suitable local service is\n   already available.\n\n   This document specifies a DNS-Based Service Discovery (DNS-SD)\n   profile for discovering LLM API services on a local link.  An\n   advertiser publishes the service location and a small set of API\n   connection parameters.  A discovery client resolves these records and\n   offers the resulting connection information to an AI application or\n   its configuration adapter.  Subsequent LLM API requests are sent\n   directly to the discovered service, not through the advertiser or\n   discovery client.  Discovery does not establish trust, distribute\n   credentials, select models, or define an application's configuration\n   file format.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-05T10:38:23Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 162124, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-cui-dawn-local-llm-api-discovery", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 13, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-cui-dawn-local-llm-api-discovery/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/171610/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-11-01T10:38:23Z", "title": "Discovery of LLM API Services in Local Networks", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}]}